  "solutions": [
    {
      "title": "Brute Force",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n log n) | Space: O(n)",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function topKFrequent(nums, k) {\n  const counts = new Map();\n  for (const x of nums) {\n    counts.set(x, (counts.get(x) || 0) + 1);\n  }\n  const entries = Array.from(counts.entries());\n  entries.sort((a, b) => b[1] !== a[1] ? b[1] - a[1] : a[0] - b[0]);\n  return entries.slice(0, k).map(x => x[0]);\n}",
      "isReference": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Optimal",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n) | Space: O(n)",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function topKFrequent(nums, k) {\n  const counts = new Map();\n  for (const x of nums) {\n    counts.set(x, (counts.get(x) || 0) + 1);\n  }\n  const buckets = Array.from({ length: nums.length + 1 }, () => []);\n  for (const [val, freq] of counts.entries()) {\n    buckets[freq].push(val);\n  }\n  const res = [];\n  for (let f = nums.length; f >= 1 && res.length < k; f--) {\n    if (buckets[f].length > 0) {\n      buckets[f].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n      for (const val of buckets[f]) {\n        res.push(val);\n        if (res.length === k) break;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return res;\n}",
      "isReference": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Brute Force",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n log n) | Space: O(n)",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def topKFrequent(nums, k):\n    from collections import Counter\n    counts = Counter(nums)\n    entries = list(counts.items())\n    entries.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[1], x[0]))\n    return [x[0] for x in entries[:k]]\n",
      "isReference": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Optimal",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n) | Space: O(n)",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def topKFrequent(nums, k):\n    from collections import Counter\n    counts = Counter(nums)\n    n = len(nums)\n    buckets = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    for val, freq in counts.items():\n        buckets[freq].append(val)\n    res = []\n    for f in range(n, 0, -1):\n        if len(res) >= k:\n            break\n        if buckets[f]:\n            buckets[f].sort()\n            for val in buckets[f]:\n                res.append(val)\n                if len(res) == k:\n                    break\n    return res\n",
      "isReference": true
    }
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute-force approach counts frequencies with a Counter, sorts all (value, frequency) pairs by frequency descending then value ascending, and returns the first k, yielding O(n log n) time. The optimal approach uses bucket sort: place each value into a bucket indexed by its frequency, then iterate from the highest frequency downward until k values are collected.",
    "why_optimal": "Bucket sort avoids sorting all distinct values\u2014when k is small, we stop early after scanning only the top buckets. On 22 test cases, PAF measured the brute-force at 0.43 ms and the optimal at 0.98 ms; the optimal's extra overhead (bucket array allocation) makes it slower than the brute on small inputs with few distinct values, but it scales better as n grows.",
    "pitfalls": "Tie-breaking must sort values ascending within the same frequency bucket, or the output order won't match the expected result. The brute force uses (-frequency, value) as the sort key; the bucket approach must replicate this within each non-empty bucket. Forgetting to sort within buckets produces non-deterministic ordering."
  },
